lines=['# Combined IoT security architecture: compact live study','', '**The combined architecture was implemented and tested in closed loop in Cooja.** The gateway consumes actual authenticated Z1 observations, runs host detection and network evidence/repair decisions, and sends authenticated UDP queries and repair grants that execute on the motes. This report covers42accepted final runs and4source/calibration runs; bring-up attempts are excluded.','',f"The combined system detected {sum(r['host_episode_detected'] for r in attack)}/{len(attack)} configured host-misuse episodes. Its two complete benign scenarios produced {sum(r['benign_alarm_observations'] for r in ben)}/{sum(r['benign_scored_observations'] for r in ben)} alarmed scored observations. Sparse-burst coverage was {sum(r['bursts_with_alarm_within_15s'] for r in sparse)}/{sum(r['executed_cpu_bursts'] for r in sparse)}. An episode-level hit must not be interpreted as coverage of every burst. Pre-attack warnings are separately recorded; an already-active warning is not evidence of a new onset detection.",'','## Comparison and ablation table','','Each row aggregates the same six deployment/context cells. PDR is unique delivered genuine application work divided by all offered work, including blocked/unreachable offers. Worst-source PDR is calculated per run then averaged. Communication is actual attempted application payload, including mandatory host/resource reports, periodic source-work reports, queries/replies, authorization and repair copies. It is not PHY airtime, energy or a cap on adversarial traffic.','', '| Policy | Runs | Mean delivery % | Mean worst-source % | Mean defense payload B | Host episodes hit | Benign alarms/scored | Sparse bursts covered |','|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|']
